Q: Combine the output of proc freq A: <p>Using CATS() removes (strips) the leading and trailing spaces from the items listed.  If you want the spaces then use something that preserves the spaces.</p>
<p>Here are some examples.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code> 73         data have;
 74           n=3;
 75           percent = 15;
 76           length str1-str4 $20 ;
 77           str1 = cats(N, ' (', put(Percent, 5.1), '%)');
 78           str2 = catx(' ',n,'(',put(percent,5.1),'%)');
 79           str3 = catx(' ',n,cat('(',put(percent,5.1),'%)'));
 80           str4 = catx(' ',n,cats('(',put(percent,5.1),'%)'));
 81           put (_all_) (=/);
 82         run;

 n=3
 percent=15
 str1=3(15.0%)
 str2=3 ( 15.0 %)
 str3=3 ( 15.0%)
 str4=3 (15.0%)
</code></pre>
<p>Notice that the value 15 is displayed as ` 15.0` by the 5.1 format.</p>
<p>STR3 looks like what you requested (but it is hard to tell since you did not post it using a fixed space font.)</p>
<p>STR4 looks more like what I have seen used for that type of report.</p>

Q: Is there a PHPDoc standard to describe function attributes passed by reference? A: <p>@Tgr is correct. It should be noted that both <code>@param</code> and <code>@param-out</code> are necessary for by-reference variables. If the input and output types are the same a duplication is required.</p>
<p>Synthetic example:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>/**
 * @param string $s Input string.
 * @param-out string $s Trimmed and upper case string.
 *
 * @return bool True if string `$s` was mutated.
 */
function strTrimAndToUpper(string &amp;$s): bool
{
    $sOriginal = $s;
    $s = strtoupper(trim($s));
    return $s !== $sOriginal;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How can I add additional functionality to a class? A: <p>If you have access to your object you want to change then all you need is to use the corresponding function of text and put it into eg. a function in your button class:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>class Button
{
    sf::Text text;

    // ...

    void setColor(sf::Color color)
    {
        text.setOutlineColor(color);
    }

    // ...
</code></pre>
<p>Same goes with any other function predefined in the sfml library for drawable objects</p>
